# Intent: Train 4 item 2, word reactions (stock: saying hello shows happy, love dances). A data table of up to 40 rows, each a few words mapped to a face, a quiet sound, a face extra and optionally a built-in dance. Built in: hello, thanks, thank you, love, sorry, good night and their pairs in Spanish, French, German, Portuguese and Italian. Matching folds the text to lower-case ASCII with accents removed (the sharp s is ss), looks for whole words and takes the longest phrase, so 'sorry' does not match 'sorrow' and 'thank you' beats 'you'. A line the person said or the unit is saying is checked at the caption hook, whatever the captions setting says; a reaction plays at most every 4 seconds and the same phrase at most every 20. The table is one small record in its own storage namespace (checksummed; a damaged one falls back to the built-in table), about 1.1 KB of RAM. Reactions only use the quiet sounds (not the alarm-like ones), follow the sound policy and the hard mute, and do nothing in a mute, vision-off or quiet look. The face is drawn straight on the avatar (no sleeping state) and goes back to the resting face unless another emotion came in. self.robot.dance now calls one shared function. Tools self.reaction.get_table, set_table, reset and test.
# Placement: unit (the table and the matching live on the robot and work with the host off; the settings page edits the table)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/board/stackchan_display.cc, firmware/main/hal/hal_mcp.cpp of the StackChan main component (MIT)
# Check after a flash: self.reaction.test with 'hello' answers the sparkles row; 'good morning' matches nothing until set_table adds it; a bad row answers 'row N: ...' and changes nothing; reset brings back the built-in table; with the unit unmuted, 'thanks' shows the happy face with sparkles for a few seconds.
# Kconfig: MAIPAI_REACT (default y); off removes the table, the tools and the hook.
